k they may be incentivized and  especially other central banks may be  incentivized to do that if they continue  to see uh  bitcoin exclusively as just a digital  reserve asset so without opening a can  of worms uh two of the countries that i  could easily see uh being very  interested who have publicly stated  they'd like to get off the dollar system  uh but who have ultimately failed to do  it up until now is russia and china uh  do you think that it's more likely that  maybe uh a competitor to america uh  would do it or do you think it's more  likely that the us like like i'm very  fascinated by this idea of like the  global game theory and when somebody  like el salvador with six and a half  million people does it i think there's  some people who say oh like that that's  an inflection point that that's  interesting there's a lot of other  people who just say hey they got six and  a half million people there's kind of  consolidated power uh you essentially  have a president and his brothers who  are younger they're forward thinking  from a technology perspective and so you  know it kind of makes sense  it's very different if the rumors around  brazil potentially doing something we've  seen some legislation uh  kind of presented or if the united  states was to do it or russia or china  and some of these like large large  countries with hundreds of millions of  people  so how do you just think about uh you  know i guess like the the competition  among the super powers the people who  actually have very large citizen bases  and frankly have just large economies  that uh this isn't such a simple uh  decision it's just like hey we're gonna  buy somewhere to get 30 everyone who  downloads the wallet and if we screw it  up well we're dollarized country and it  may not be as uh kind of uh big of an  issue given that we wouldn't really be  risking our own currency like how do you  think about that  well if you've got a brazil and so  you're talking about the central bank 